 ## Operating characteristics
-
-*   **Time Complexity:** Training requires approximately two days on an NVIDIA 1080Ti GPU [S1]. Inference is fast as it relies on a feed-forward GAN, followed by a DSS optimization step.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the image resolution and batch size for the GAN training. The point cloud size is 20K points for training data generation [S1].
-*   **Measured Performance:**
-    *   On 1% noisy, 20K point input: Pix2Pix-DSS outperforms WLOP, EAR, RIMLS, GPF, and PointCleanNet quantitatively and qualitatively [S1].
-    *   On 0.3% noisy, 100K point input: Pix2Pix-DSS reconstructs clean surfaces with abundant hallucinated details, outperforming PointCleanNet which deteriorates due to generalizability issues [S1].
-*   **Strengths:**
-    *   Can hallucinate plausible high-frequency details that optimization-based methods miss [S1].
-    *   Less sensitive to point sampling characteristics compared to direct learning-based methods [S1].
-    *   Simultaneous multi-view optimization in DSS resolves cross-view inconsistencies [S1].
-*   **Limitations:**
-    *   Not trained with view-consistency constraints, potentially causing small inconsistencies in high-frequency detail regions across views [S1].
-    *   Requires a large dataset for training (Sketchfab dataset) [S1].
-    *   Limited to the noise levels it was trained on (1.0% and 0.3%) [S1].
-*   **When to use:** When high-frequency detail hallucination is desired and the noise level is within the trained range (0.3% or 1.0%).
-*   **When not to use:** When view-consistency is critical and cannot be resolved by DSS, or when the noise level is significantly different from the training distribution.
